  "id": "F1",
  "file": "src/util/proc.ts",
  "line": 302,
  "title": "An identity-inspection failure after SIGSTOP leaves the frozen process stopped forever",
  "detail": "On any error, the catch block re-runs processBirth for each claimed member before its SIGKILL, inside `try { \u2026 } catch {}`. If that inspection throws as well (proc_pidinfo failing with an errno other than ESRCH, for example), the SIGKILL is silently skipped and the member stays in state T. That breaks the code's own rule, 'Never leave a frozen member behind'. Reproduction (Codex, temporary test with an owned /bin/sleep and real signal delivery): make darwinProcessBirth throw for that PID after its SIGSTOP. runProcess rejects, the recorded signals are only ['SIGSTOP'], and ps shows the sleeper in state T. Fix: never leave a member that this sweep froze in the stopped state. A process this sweep stopped can't exit by itself, so while it stays stopped its PID can't be reused. If its identity can't be re-confirmed after bounded retries, still SIGKILL the members this sweep froze, and report the inspection failure in the thrown error. Keep refusing to signal any PID this sweep never froze. Add a test with injected inspection failures (thrown after the freeze, and again in the error path) that ends with no stopped member and an error naming the failure."
 },
 {
  "id": "F2",
  "file": "src/util/proc.ts",
  "line": 255,
  "title": "'kept growing' is reported for ordinary discovery of an existing tree",
  "detail": "`growing` becomes true as soon as any PID is claimed after the first populated scan, so a tree that existed before cleanup started, but was discovered over two scans, is reported as 'set kept growing'. Reproduction (Codex): start two sleepers before cleanup, expose one in the first injected snapshot and both afterwards, suppress their signals and advance the clock to the deadline. The error reports 2 processes and 'set kept growing', although neither forked. #537 asks for this to tell a forking tree apart from a slow machine. Fix: count growth from process birth times, using members born after the cleanup started (processBirth already returns the start time), instead of from discovery order. Add that case as a test."
 }
